<p>Sometimes a tooth can't be saved. At 3rd Set Smiles in Tempe, Dr. Matthew Phillips removes it comfortably, in-house, and helps you plan what replaces it, so you leave with a next step rather than just a gap.</p>

<h2>Why a tooth might need to come out</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Severe decay that's beyond what a filling or crown can restore</li>
  <li>A cracked or split tooth that can't be restored</li>
  <li>Advanced gum disease that has destroyed the supporting bone</li>
  <li>Impacted or problematic wisdom teeth (see <a href="/services/wisdom-teeth-removal/">wisdom teeth removal</a>)</li>
  <li>Crowding that needs to be relieved for orthodontic treatment</li>
</ul>

<h2>Simple vs. surgical extractions</h2>
<p>A simple extraction is for a tooth that's fully erupted and accessible — most adult teeth fall into this category. A surgical extraction is needed when a tooth is broken below the gumline, impacted (like many wisdom teeth), or requires a small bone window for access. Dr. Phillips performs both in-office, so you don't have to be referred out.</p>

<h2>Bone preservation &amp; planning ahead</h2>
<p>Once a tooth is removed, the bone that used to support it starts to shrink. If you may want an implant down the road, we typically place a small bone graft into the socket at the time of extraction to preserve the site. We'll discuss this option with you before the procedure.</p>

<h2>Comfort during the visit</h2>
<p>Local anesthesia is the standard. For longer procedures or anxious patients, we offer <a href="/services/sedation-dentistry/">sedation options</a> so you can rest comfortably through the visit.</p>

<h2>What happens at your visit</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li><strong>Exam and X-ray</strong> to see the roots, the surrounding bone, and whether the tooth can be saved after all.</li>
  <li><strong>A clear plan and price</strong> before anything starts, including what will replace the tooth.</li>
  <li><strong>The extraction</strong>, with the area fully numbed and sedation available if you want it.</li>
  <li><strong>After-care instructions</strong> to protect the socket while it heals.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Same-day extractions are available.</strong> If a tooth needs to come out, Dr. Phillips can often do it at the same visit, so you don't have to come back another day.</p>
<!-- Confirmed by Mark 2026-09-30: same-day extractions are offered. -->

<h2>Recovery</h2>
<p>Most people feel mostly normal within two to three days. For the first few days, avoid straws, spitting, and smoking, and stick to soft foods; these protect the blood clot and help prevent <a href="/blog/dry-socket-prevention-and-symptoms/">dry socket</a>. Call us if pain gets worse after day three instead of better.</p>

<h2>Replacing the tooth</h2>
<p>Planning the replacement at the same time saves bone and time. Options include a <a href="/services/dental-implants/">dental implant</a>, a bridge, or a partial denture. For several failing teeth, see <a href="/blog/same-day-extractions-and-implants/">whether a tooth extraction and implant can happen the same day</a> and <a href="/services/all-on-4/">All-on-4 full-arch implants</a>.</p>

